ts for Pappas, including NYT/Siena and UNH surveys", "Pappas holds a large fundraising edge ($15.4M raised vs $4.2M) even as outside groups spend heavily for Sununu", "Prediction markets and most raters favor Democrats, though Cook and Inside Elections still call it a Tossup", "Open seat in a Democratic-leaning state, but Sununu's moderate brand and famous name keep Republicans viable"], "rationale": "Polls from late September consistently show Pappas ahead by five to eight points, prediction markets price him near 86 percent, and he has vastly out-raised Sununu; Sununu's strength with moderates and heavy outside spending argue for only a modest haircut to the polling picture.", "watch": "A late consolidation of independents behind Sununu, amplified by outside spending in a race that could decide Senate control, would tighten this considerably.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Jeanne Shaheen (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "New Hampshire's open Senate seat pits Congressman Chris Pappas against former Governor John Sununu, with Jeanne Shaheen retiring. Polls since mid-September consistently show Pappas ahead, with high-quality surveys from the New York Times/Siena and the University of New Hampshire giving him leads of five to eight points. Prediction markets put his chances near 86 percent, and most rating outlets place the race in the Likely or Lean Democratic column, though Cook and Inside Elections call it a Tossup.\n\nPappas has outraised Sununu substantially, though outside groups have spent heavily to aid the Republican, and coverage describes a contest drawing national attention because it could decide Senate control. New Hampshire leans slightly Democratic, but Sununu's moderate record and famous name keep him competitive. The forecast expects Pappas to win by roughly six points, with real uncertainty about whether the race tightens in the final weeks.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8721, "margin": 5.76}, {"date": "2